Build the frame limiter in the Emulator constructor, not only in Run()

Emulator::ProcessEndOfFrame() dereferences _frameLimiter unconditionally, and
that object is created in exactly one place: Emulator::Run(). A frontend that
drives the core one frame at a time never calls Run(), yet it cannot avoid
ProcessEndOfFrame() either, because the consoles call it themselves at the end of
every frame (SnesPpu::SendFrame by way of SnesConsole::ProcessEndOfFrame, and
NesPpu directly). The result is a null dereference on the first frame.

Constructing the limiter with a zero delay costs nothing and changes no
behaviour for Mesen's own frontends: Run() still replaces it with one built from
the real frame delay, and a zero delay never waits.

Retro Pal applies this patch at build time (Vendor/mesen-ios/build.sh) rather
than forking the core, so the submodule stays pinned to upstream and this file is
the whole of our modification. It is worth sending upstream; if it lands, this
patch and the step in build.sh both go away.

--- a/Core/Shared/Emulator.cpp
+++ b/Core/Shared/Emulator.cpp
@@ -77,5 +77,12 @@
 	_debugRequestCount = 0;
 	_blockDebuggerRequestCount = 0;
 
+	//The frame limiter is otherwise built only by Run(). A frontend that steps
+	//frames itself never calls Run(), while the consoles call
+	//Emulator::ProcessEndOfFrame() on their own every frame, which dereferences
+	//it. Building it here costs nothing: Run() replaces it with the real delay,
+	//and a zero delay never waits.
+	_frameLimiter.reset(new FrameLimiter(0));
+
 	_videoDecoder->Init();
 }
